<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Newar people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Newars</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Newars are the historic inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepal</a>, speaking Nepal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a> (Newari), a Sino-Tibetan language, and following both <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a> and Buddhist traditions. Under the Malla kings their cities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, Patan and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhaktapur">Bhaktapur</a> flourished as centres of trade, temple architecture, metalwork and painting. Newar merchants long dominated trade between Nepal and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/newar-people.jpg" alt="Newar people" width="320" height="256" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Krish Dulal · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bel_Marriage02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Newars, who call themselves Newa, are primarily inhabitants of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> and its surroundings, and the creators of its historic civilisation. The valley and nearby territories formed the Newar kingdom of the Nepal Mandala. The Newars are regarded as a nation community with a relict identity, derived from an ethnically diverse, previously existing polity. They descend from the varied peoples who have lived there since prehistoric times, together with Indo-Aryan tribes such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a>, Kosala and Mallas, who arrived at different periods and merged with the local population by marriage and by adopting its language and customs.</p><p>A <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> inscription of 512 at Tistung, west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu">Kathmandu</a>, contains the phrase greetings to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nepal">Nepals</a>, showing that Nepal named both country and people. Newar rule in the Nepal Mandala ended with its conquest by the Gorkha Kingdom in 1768.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>For over two millennia the Newa civilisation preserved a microcosm of classical North Indian culture in which Brahmanic and Buddhist elements had equal standing. The Malla dynasty patronised the Maithili language, giving it equal status with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sanskrit">Sanskrit</a> at court, and invited Maithil Brahmin priests, many of whom settled in the valley. People arriving from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a> in the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tirhut">Tirhut</a> in the south added to the diversity and shaped the dominant culture.</p><p>The economy rested on trade, industry and agriculture, with social groups tied to hereditary professions providing ritual and economic services. Towns and villages specialised in particular goods, and rich farming produced a surplus for export. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caste">caste</a> system was first introduced under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/licchavi">Licchavis</a> but took its present shape in the medieval Malla period.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Nepal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bhasa">Bhasa</a>, a Tibeto-Burman language, binds the Newars together. Its literature began as prose in the fourteenth century with a law book of 1380 and a chronicle of 1389. Mahendra Malla, who reigned from 1560 to 1574, is known as the first poet of the language, and its dramas were written mostly in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries. It was written in scripts descended from Brahmi, including Ranjana and Bhujimol. According to the 2021 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/census">census</a>, 89.1 percent of Newars were <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindu</a>, 9.0 percent Buddhist and 1.9 percent Christian, and both traditions share festivals and worship deities of both.</p><p>Newar art is primarily religious. Its paubha painting, sculpture and metalwork in lost-wax casting are well known, and the earliest dated paubha, the Vasudhara Mandala, was painted in 1365. Brick buildings with carved windows and doors characterise the architecture, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathmandu-valley">Kathmandu Valley</a> holds seven <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a> and 2,500 temples and shrines. The windows reached their peak in the mid-eighteenth century. Festivals include jatras, processions of a car or portable shrine, and masked dances.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Newar people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…Narayan Shah, who became king of Gorkha in 1743, set out to bring under his rule the many principalities of the central Himalaya, among them the western confederations known as the Chaubisi and Baisi kingdoms and the three <b>Newar</b> kingdoms of the valley: Kathmandu, Lalitpur (Patan) and Bhaktapur.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-nepal">Kingdom of Nepal · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Bhaktapur was the most traditional of the three <b>Newar</b> kingdoms, rich in festivals and crafts such as pottery and woodcarving.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bhaktapur">Kingdom of Bhaktapur</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Newar</b> artisans produced fine metal images and painted scrolls, and the cult of the living goddess Kumari was associated with the court.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-kantipur">Kingdom of Kantipur</a></li><li><blockquote>Its kings intermarried with the Gorkha and Tanahun royal families, and the town of Tansen developed <b>Newar</b> trading communities and temples.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-palpa">Kingdom of Palpa</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Newar</b> artisans produced metalwork, painting and architecture for Hindu and Buddhist patrons, and the valley profited from trade between India and Tibet.</blockquote><a href="/state/malla-dynasty-nepal">Malla dynasty (Nepal)</a></li></ul></section>
